# Key Lesson Moments: Context and Risk Matter

## Lesson 8: AI Privacy and Safety

<img 
src="https://images.code.org/1d813120ea57078937abdf39ded8cc84-image-1779249540057.jpeg" 
alt="" 
style="width:35%; border-radius:10px; margin:0px 0px 10px 15px; float:right;">

<a href="https://studio.code.org/courses/ai-discoveries-2026/units/1/lessons/8" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lesson Plan Link</a>

📎 **Student Resource**: <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1LIw4YHQI0oTthaV0WMDd3pu_yw1fG5Xt2g8XCpulaWY/edit?usp=sharing" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy and Safety Exploration</a>

**Lesson Objectives**: 

* Analyze potential consequences of oversharing in an AI-enabled world.
* Distinguish between public, private, and sensitive information.
* Explain how AI systems use pattern recognition to create detailed output.

### Student Activity: Z-Tech's Selfie

This lesson begins with a mystery scenario: a famous streamer named Z-Tech suddenly stops posting online after strangers identify where he lives using only details from a selfie he shared publicly.

Students examine the selfie and search for “invisible” clues that could reveal information about Z-Tech’s location or identity. As they investigate, students begin realizing that:

* small pieces of information can combine to create major privacy risks
* AI systems can help people identify patterns humans might overlook
* information that feels harmless in isolation can become risky in context

As students discuss their findings, they begin moving toward more nuanced thinking about privacy, safety, and responsible information sharing online.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

